.SH Making a String
.PP
A \fBString\fR you get from the str (see ktm-str(3)) module is yours, to free when you are done. \fBscoped\fR frees it at the end of the block.
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
import str;

void main() {
    scoped String s = str.from("Hello");
    s.append(", world");
    s.appendFormat(" %d", 2026);
    printf("%s (%d bytes)\en", s, s.len);
}
.EE
.RE
.PP
Output:
.RS 4
.EX
Hello, world 2026 (17 bytes)
.EE
.RE
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
import str;

void main() {
    scoped String a = str.new();               // empty, nothing allocated yet
    scoped String b = str.from("copied");      // a copy of any text
    scoped String c = str.withCap(64);         // empty, with room for 64 bytes
    scoped String d = str.format("%d\-%s", 42, "x");
    String e;                              // a declared String starts empty
    a.append("grown");
    printf("[%s] [%s] [%s] [%s] [%d]\en", a, b, c, d, e.len);
}
.EE
.RE
.PP
Output:
.RS 4
.EX
[grown] [copied] [] [42\-x] [0]
.EE
.RE
.PP
\fBstr.format\fR takes \fBprintf\fR\&'s format and returns the result as a new \fBString\fR:
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
import str;

void main() {
    scoped String line = str.format("%\-6s|%5.1f|%03d", "id", 3.14159, 7);
    printf("[%s]\en", line);
}
.EE
.RE
.PP
Output:
.RS 4
.EX
[id    |  3.1|007]
.EE
.RE
.SH Changing a String
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
import str;

void main() {
    scoped String s = str.from("found 3 files");
    s.insert(0, "> ");
    s.replace("files", "logs");
    s.append(" in /tmp");
    s.appendByte('!');
    printf("%s\en", s);
    s.remove(0, 2);
    s.toUpper();
    printf("%s\en", s);
    s.reverse();
    printf("%s\en", s);
    s.clear();
    printf("[%s] %d\en", s, s.len);
}
.EE
.RE
.PP
Output:
.RS 4
.EX
> found 3 logs in /tmp!
FOUND 3 LOGS IN /TMP!
!PMT/ NI SGOL 3 DNUOF
[] 0
.EE
.RE
.IP \(bu 2
\fBs.remove(a, b)\fR removes the bytes from \fBa\fR up to, not including, \fBb\fR\&.
.IP \(bu 2
\fBs.replace(old, new)\fR replaces every \fBold\fR\&.
.IP \(bu 2
The length and capacity fields can't be assigned: the methods keep them right.
.PP
Building text in a loop:
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
import str;

void main() {
    scoped String csv = str.new();
    i64 values[] = {3, 1, 4, 1, 5};
    for (i64 i = 0; i < 5; i++) {
        if (i > 0) { csv.appendByte(','); }
        csv.appendFormat("%d", values[i]);
    }
    printf("%s\en", csv);
}
.EE
.RE
.PP
Output:
.RS 4
.EX
3,1,4,1,5
.EE
.RE
.SH Reading text
.PP
A \fBString\fR converts to a \fBu8[]\fR, and so does a literal. So every function that reads text takes either.
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
import str;

void main() {
    scoped String s = str.from("Hello, world");
    u8[] hello = s[0:5];
    printf("%s %c %d\en", hello, s[7], s.len);
    printf("%d %d %d\en", s.startsWith("Hell"), s.find("world"), s.contains("xyz"));
    printf("%d %d\en", s.equals("Hello, world"), s.endsWith("!"));
}
.EE
.RE
.PP
Output:
.RS 4
.EX
Hello w 12
1 7 0
1 0
.EE
.RE
.PP
The functions that read text are in str (see ktm-str(3)), and can be called as methods on a \fBu8[]\fR or a \fBString\fR: \fBs.find("x")\fR is \fBstr.find(s, "x")\fR\&.
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
import str;

void main() {
    u8[] line = "  name = ktm  ";
    u8[] t = str.trim(line);
    i64 eq = t.find("=");
    u8[] key = str.trim(t[0:eq]);
    u8[] value = str.trim(t[eq + 1:]);
    printf("[%s] [%s]\en", key, value);
}
.EE
.RE
.PP
Output:
.RS 4
.EX
[name] [ktm]
.EE
.RE
.SS Splitting and joining
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
import str;

void main() {
    scoped u8[][] parts = str.split("red,green,,blue", ",");
    printf("%d parts:", parts.len);
    for (auto p : parts) {
        printf(" [%s]", p);
    }
    printf("\en");
    scoped String joined = str.join(parts, " + ");
    printf("%s\en", joined);
}
.EE
.RE
.PP
Output:
.RS 4
.EX
4 parts: [red] [green] [] [blue]
red + green +  + blue
.EE
.RE
.PP
The parts are views into the text that was split: the list is yours to free, the parts are not.
.SS Numbers in text
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
import str;

void main() {
    i64 n;
    f64 f;
    if (str.toInt("42", &n)) {
        printf("%d\en", n + 1);
    }
    if (!str.toInt("42x", &n)) {
        printf("42x isn't an integer\en");
    }
    if (str.toFloat("2.5", &f)) {
        printf("%.2f\en", f * 2);
    }
    scoped String back = str.format("%d", 1234);
    printf("%s has %d digits\en", back, back.len);
}
.EE
.RE
.PP
Output:
.RS 4
.EX
43
42x isn't an integer
5.00
1234 has 4 digits
.EE
.RE
.SH Copies share bytes
.PP
Assigning a \fBString\fR copies its three fields, so both variables point at the same bytes, and only one of them may free them. \fBclone()\fR makes a copy with bytes of its own.
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
import str;

void main() {
    scoped String a = str.from("shared");
    String alias = a;              // the same bytes: don't free both
    scoped String own = a.clone();     // bytes of its own
    own.toUpper();
    printf("%s %s %s\en", a, alias, own);
}
.EE
.RE
.PP
Output:
.RS 4
.EX
shared shared SHARED
.EE
.RE
.IP \(bu 2
\fBfree()\fR empties the \fBString\fR it is called on, so freeing the same variable twice is harmless.
.IP \(bu 2
A view (\fBs[a:b]\fR) is valid until the \fBString\fR changes or is freed: growing may move its bytes.
.IP \(bu 2
Freeing through two copies is a double free, which \fB\-jit \-Memsafe\fR reports.
.SH Bytes, not characters
.PP
\fBlen\fR and \fBs[i]\fR count bytes. Text is UTF-8 by convention, so a character outside ASCII takes more than one byte.
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
import str;

void main() {
    u8[] word = "naïve";
    printf("%d bytes, %d characters\en", word.len, str.runeCount(word));
}
.EE
.RE
.PP
Output:
.RS 4
.EX
6 bytes, 5 characters
.EE
.RE
.SH Literals are read-only
.PP
A string literal's bytes can't be changed. A write the compiler can see is an error that says what to do; one it can't see crashes at run time. Copy the text into a \fBString\fR first.
.PP
This does not compile:
.RS 4
.EX
void main() {
    u8[] a = "abc";
    a[0] = 'x';
}
.EE
.RE
.PP
Output:
.RS 4
.EX
read\-only
.EE
.RE
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
import str;

void main() {
    scoped String a = str.from("abc");
    a[0] = 'x';
    printf("%s\en", a);
}
.EE
.RE
.PP
Output:
.RS 4
.EX
xbc
.EE
.RE
.PP
A \fBu8\fR array initialised from a literal gets its own writable copy:
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
void main() {
    u8 buf[] = "abc";        // 4 bytes: a, b, c and the NUL
    u8 name[8] = "hi";       // the 6 bytes after "hi" are zero
    buf[0] = 'X';
    printf("%s %d %s %d\en", buf, sizeof(buf), name, sizeof(name));
}
.EE
.RE
.PP
Output:
.RS 4
.EX
Xbc 4 hi 8
.EE
.RE
.SH C strings
.PP
A C string is a \fBu8 *\fR to bytes that end in a NUL: what C functions take and return. A \fBString\fR\&'s bytes always end in a NUL, so a \fBString\fR variable goes where a \fBu8 *\fR is expected as it is, with no copy. \fBcstr.view(p)\fR turns a C string into a \fBu8[]\fR\&.
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
import cstr, libc, str;

void main() {
    scoped String s = str.from("for C");
    u8 *c = s;
    u8[] back = cstr.view(c);
    printf("%s %d %d\en", c, back.len, libc.strlen(s));
}
.EE
.RE
.PP
Output:
.RS 4
.EX
for C 5 5
.EE
.RE
.IP \(bu 2
The pointer is the \fBString\fR\&'s own buffer: valid until the \fBString\fR changes or is freed.
.IP \(bu 2
A \fBString\fR that a call returns doesn't convert (nothing would free it): keep it in a variable first.
.IP \(bu 2
The cstr (see ktm-cstr(3)) module has the classic C string functions.
.SH printf and %s
.PP
\fB%s\fR takes a C string, a \fBu8[]\fR or a \fBString\fR\&. A width works (\fB%\-10s\fR); a precision cuts the text.
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
import str;

void main() {
    scoped String s = str.from("ktm");
    u8[] part = "language"[0:4];
    printf("[%\-6s][%6s][%s][%.2s]\en", s, part, "c string", "abcdef");
}
.EE
.RE
.PP
Output:
.RS 4
.EX
[ktm   ][  lang][c string][ab]
.EE
.RE
.SH Which one to use
.IP \(bu 2
Take text as a \fBu8[]\fR parameter: callers can pass a literal, a slice or a \fBString\fR\&.
.IP \(bu 2
Return a \fBString\fR when you make new text, and say in the comment that the caller frees it.
.IP \(bu 2
Keep a \fBString\fR in a struct for text the struct owns, and free it in the struct's \fBfree()\fR\&.
.IP \(bu 2
Use \fBu8 *\fR only to talk to C.
